\f0\fs30 \cf0 \strokec2 Have you ever noticed how tension can build up throughout the day?
\f1\fs24 \

\f0\fs30 Here's a simple technique from the Amari Method that you can try right now:
\f1\fs24 \

\f0\fs30 It\'92s called Spring Step. Please wear athletic shoes for this:
\f1\fs24 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls10\ilvl0
\f0\fs30 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Place your heels on the edge of a step, or a curb if you\'92re outside
\fs29\fsmilli14667 \
\ls10\ilvl0
\fs30 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 This looks like a calf stretch at first, but we\'92re going to turn it into a more dynamic/whole body reset
\fs29\fsmilli14667 \
\ls10\ilvl0
\fs30 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Start with your right heel and drop it off the step as far as you can - so that you feel a good stretch in your calf and Achilles tendon (your other knee will be bent and relaxed)
\fs29\fsmilli14667 \
\ls10\ilvl0
\fs30 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Start to bounce your heel, focusing on the bottom of the bounce
\fs29\fsmilli14667 \
\ls10\ilvl0
\fs30 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Think about turning your Achilles tendon into a trampoline spring
\fs29\fsmilli14667 \
\ls10\ilvl0
\fs30 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The more you bounce your heel, the more buoyant, your whole body will feel
\fs29\fsmilli14667 \
\ls10\ilvl0
\fs30 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Do this for as long as intuitively feels right (approx 20 bounces)
\fs29\fsmilli14667 \
\ls10\ilvl0
\fs30 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Then switch to the left heel and do the same thing
\fs29\fsmilli14667 \
\ls10\ilvl0
\fs30 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 If you\'92d like to add a strengthening component raise both feet up into a double caf\'e9s before starting over again if not, just keep going back-and-forth between bouncing your right and then your left heel separately
\fs29\fsmilli14667 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\ls10\ilvl0
\fs30 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Do this twice a day, approximately 3 to 4 minutes at a time
\fs29\fsmilli14667 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs30 \cf0 \strokec2 Try to keep your body upright while doing this. The bouncing action will travel up your body and you\'92ll start to feel your whole back, and your shoulder blades, relaxing more. Focus on your breath in your nose/out your mouth. When you\'92re done slowly walk around and notice the difference from head to toe.
\f1\fs24 \

